### What is binary search?

Binary search is the fundamental logarithmic-time algorithm for finding a target in a sorted array. It works by maintaining two bounds, `low` and `high`, that define the current search window, and on each step it looks at the middle element to decide which half to discard. Because each comparison eliminates half the remaining range, searching an array of n elements takes at most log2(n) steps.

At the National Bank of India, Rajesh authenticates customer logins by looking up account numbers stored in a sorted array. Binary search confirms whether an account exists and returns its index in about 20 steps for a million accounts.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class BinarySearch {

    public static int search(int[] accounts, int target) {
        int low = 0;
        int high = accounts.length - 1;

        while (low <= high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;

            if (accounts[mid] == target) {
                return mid;
            } else if (accounts[mid] < target) {
                low = mid + 1;
            } else {
                high = mid - 1;
            }
        }
        return -1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] accounts = {1001, 1025, 1050, 1100, 1200, 1350, 1500};
        int target = 1100;
        int index = search(accounts, target);
        System.out.println("Account " + target + " found at index: " + index);
    }
}
```

```text
Account 1100 found at index: 3
```

### Why does binary search require sorted data?

The correctness of binary search depends entirely on the sorted order. When `arr[mid] < target`, we know the target cannot be anywhere to the left of mid because all those elements are even smaller. This lets us safely discard the entire left half. If the array were unsorted, the target could be anywhere and no comparison at mid would tell us which half to discard.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class SortedPrecondition {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] unsorted = {1200, 1001, 1350, 1050, 1100};
        int[] sorted = unsorted.clone();
        Arrays.sort(sorted);

        // Binary search on unsorted data gives wrong result
        System.out.println("Unsorted search for 1100: " + Arrays.binarySearch(unsorted, 1100));
        // Binary search on sorted data gives correct result
        System.out.println("Sorted search for 1100: " + Arrays.binarySearch(sorted, 1100));
    }
}
```

```text
Unsorted search for 1100: -4
Sorted search for 1100: 2
```

### How does the midpoint overflow bug work?

Computing `(low + high) / 2` can overflow a 32-bit integer when both `low` and `high` are large. For example, if `low = 1,500,000,000` and `high = 2,000,000,000`, their sum exceeds `Integer.MAX_VALUE` and wraps to a negative number. The safe form `low + (high - low) / 2` avoids this because `high - low` is always non-negative and within range.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class MidpointOverflow {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int low = 1_500_000_000;
        int high = 2_000_000_000;

        int badMid = (low + high) / 2;             // overflows
        int goodMid = low + (high - low) / 2;      // safe

        System.out.println("Bad mid (overflow):  " + badMid);
        System.out.println("Good mid (safe):     " + goodMid);
    }
}
```

```text
Bad mid (overflow):  -397483648
Good mid (safe):     1750000000
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is using `low < high` instead of `low <= high`, which skips the check when the window shrinks to a single element. Another is forgetting the `+1` or `-1` on low and high updates, which causes an infinite loop when `low == mid`.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class BinarySearchMistakes {

    // WRONG: low < high misses the single-element case
    public static int searchBuggy(int[] arr, int target) {
        int low = 0, high = arr.length - 1;
        while (low < high) {  // Bug: should be low <= high
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] == target) return mid;
            else if (arr[mid] < target) low = mid + 1;
            else high = mid - 1;
        }
        return -1;  // Misses target when low == high
    }

    // CORRECT
    public static int searchCorrect(int[] arr, int target) {
        int low = 0, high = arr.length - 1;
        while (low <= high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] == target) return mid;
            else if (arr[mid] < target) low = mid + 1;
            else high = mid - 1;
        }
        return -1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
        System.out.println("Buggy search for 50: " + searchBuggy(arr, 50));
        System.out.println("Correct search for 50: " + searchCorrect(arr, 50));
    }
}
```

```text
Buggy search for 50: -1
Correct search for 50: 4
```

### How is binary search different from linear search?

| Aspect | Linear Search | Binary Search |
|---|---|---|
| Precondition | None | Array must be sorted |
| Time complexity | O(n) | O(log n) |
| Steps for 1M elements | Up to 1,000,000 | At most 20 |
| Space complexity | O(1) | O(1) iterative, O(log n) recursive |
| Implementation | Simple loop | Needs careful midpoint and bounds |

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class LinearVsBinary {

    public static int linearSearch(int[] arr, int target) {
        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            if (arr[i] == target) return i;
        }
        return -1;
    }

    public static int binarySearch(int[] arr, int target) {
        int low = 0, high = arr.length - 1;
        while (low <= high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] == target) return mid;
            else if (arr[mid] < target) low = mid + 1;
            else high = mid - 1;
        }
        return -1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] accounts = {1001, 1025, 1050, 1100, 1200, 1350, 1500};
        System.out.println("Linear: " + linearSearch(accounts, 1350));
        System.out.println("Binary: " + binarySearch(accounts, 1350));
    }
}
```

```text
Linear: 5
Binary: 5
```

### What about the recursive form?

Binary search can also be written recursively, where each call passes the narrowed bounds. The recursive form is cleaner to read but uses O(log n) stack frames, while the iterative form uses O(1) space.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class RecursiveBinarySearch {

    public static int search(int[] arr, int target, int low, int high) {
        if (low > high) return -1;

        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
        if (arr[mid] == target) return mid;
        if (arr[mid] < target) return search(arr, target, mid + 1, high);
        return search(arr, target, low, mid - 1);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] accounts = {1001, 1025, 1050, 1100, 1200, 1350, 1500};
        int result = search(accounts, 1200, 0, accounts.length - 1);
        System.out.println("Recursive result: " + result);
    }
}
```

```text
Recursive result: 4
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include searching an empty array, searching for a value smaller than the first element, searching for a value larger than the last element, and searching an array with a single element.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class BinarySearchEdgeCases {

    public static int search(int[] arr, int target) {
        int low = 0, high = arr.length - 1;
        while (low <= high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
            if (arr[mid] == target) return mid;
            else if (arr[mid] < target) low = mid + 1;
            else high = mid - 1;
        }
        return -1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Empty array: " + search(new int[]{}, 5));
        System.out.println("Below min: " + search(new int[]{10, 20, 30}, 5));
        System.out.println("Above max: " + search(new int[]{10, 20, 30}, 50));
        System.out.println("Single element found: " + search(new int[]{42}, 42));
        System.out.println("Single element not found: " + search(new int[]{42}, 99));
    }
}
```

```text
Empty array: -1
Below min: -1
Above max: -1
Single element found: 0
Single element not found: -1
```

### How does binary search generalize to answer-space search?

Beyond searching arrays, binary search applies to optimization problems where you search on the answer space. For example, finding the minimum interest rate that satisfies a loan constraint. You binary-search on the rate itself, checking at each midpoint whether the constraint is met, and halve the range accordingly. This pattern appears in problems like "minimum capacity to ship packages" and "split array largest sum."

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.math.RoundingMode;

public class AnswerSpaceSearch {

    // Find minimum monthly EMI that pays off a loan within maxMonths
    public static BigDecimal findMinEmi(
            BigDecimal principal, BigDecimal annualRate, int maxMonths) {
        BigDecimal low = BigDecimal.ONE;
        BigDecimal high = principal;
        BigDecimal monthlyRate = annualRate.divide(
                BigDecimal.valueOf(1200), 10, RoundingMode.HALF_UP);

        while (high.subtract(low).compareTo(BigDecimal.valueOf(0.01)) > 0) {
            BigDecimal mid = low.add(high).divide(
                    BigDecimal.valueOf(2), 10, RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
            if (canPayOff(principal, monthlyRate, mid, maxMonths)) {
                high = mid;
            } else {
                low = mid;
            }
        }
        return high.setScale(2, RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
    }

    private static boolean canPayOff(
            BigDecimal principal, BigDecimal rate, BigDecimal emi, int months) {
        BigDecimal balance = principal;
        for (int i = 0; i < months && balance.compareTo(BigDecimal.ZERO) > 0; i++) {
            balance = balance.add(balance.multiply(rate)).subtract(emi);
        }
        return balance.compareTo(BigDecimal.ZERO) <= 0;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal principal = new BigDecimal("100000");
        BigDecimal annualRate = new BigDecimal("12");
        System.out.println("Min EMI for 12 months: " + findMinEmi(principal, annualRate, 12));
    }
}
```

```text
Min EMI for 12 months: 8884.88
```
